      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we start by expressing this condition mathematically. We need an integer \\(x\\) such that:\n\n\\[ x \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22} \\]\n\nThis means that \\(x\\) can be written in the form:\n\n\\[ x = 22k + 15 \\]\n\nfor some integer \\(k\\). We need to find the largest 4-digit number that fits this form. The largest 4-digit number is 9999. Therefore, we need to find the largest integer \\(k\\) such that:\n\n\\[ 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\n\nFirst, we isolate \\(k\\):\n\n\\[ 22k \\leq 9984 \\]\n\n\\[ k \\leq \\frac{9984}{22} \\]\n\nCalculating the division:\n\n\\[ k \\leq 453.818 \\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, the largest possible value for \\(k\\) is 453. We now substitute \\(k = 453\\) back into the expression for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[ x = 22 \\cdot 453 + 15 \\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(22 \\cdot 453\\):\n\n\\[ 22 \\cdot 453 = 10106 \\]\n\nThen we add 15:\n\n\\[ x = 10106 + 15 = 10121 \\]\n\nThus, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{10121} \\]",
