      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\), we need to determine the largest number \\( N \\) such that \\( N \\) satisfies the congruence relation \\( N \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22} \\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\( N \\) in terms of the congruence:\n\\[\nN = 22k + 15\n\\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is an integer.\n\nWe need \\( N \\) to be a 4-digit number, so:\n\\[\n1000 \\leq N < 10000\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( N = 22k + 15 \\) into this inequality:\n\\[\n1000 \\leq 22k + 15 < 10000\n\\]\n\nFirst, subtract 15 from all parts of the inequality:\n\\[\n985 \\leq 22k < 9985\n\\]\n\nNext, divide the entire inequality by 22:\n\\[\n\\frac{985}{22} \\leq k < \\frac{9985}{22}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the division:\n\\[\n44.7727 \\leq k < 454.7727\n\\]\n\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, the largest possible value for \\( k \\) is 454. Therefore, the largest 4-digit integer \\( N \\) is:\n\\[\nN = 22 \\times 454 + 15\n\\]\n\nNow, perform the multiplication:\n\\[\n22 \\times 454 = 10148\n\\]\n\nAdd 15 to this result:\n\\[\nN = 10148 + 15 = 10163\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest 4-digit integer congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10163}\n\\]",
